[Event "FIDE World U13 Women 2026"]
[Site "Roquetas de Mar ESP"]
[Date "2026.10.18"]
[Round "7.22"]
[White "Serradell Munoz, Silvia"]
[WhiteElo "1925"]
[Black "Tselmuun, Ider"]
[BlackElo "1902"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Tag "Upset"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – Kan Sicilian: Black's View"]
[Opening "Kan/O'Kelly: ...e5 – queenside bind after queens off"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Nf3 Bb4
{This is the heart of the Kan with ...e5: Black grabs the centre immediately and pins the knight with Bb4. The idea is that after Nxd4–Nf3, the knight has retreated and Black has already claimed space with ...e5. The a6 move, played on move 2, serves two purposes: it prevents Nb5 (a key White threat in many Sicilian lines) and supports an eventual ...b5 expansion.}
7. Bc4 d6 8. O-O Qc7 9. Qd3 Bxc3 10. bxc3 b5
{Black's plan unfolds logically. After exchanging the bishop for the c3 knight, Black plays ...b5 – the move that a6 prepared. The queenside will become Black's territory: the pawn chain a6-b5 will support a future ...Bb7 or ...a5-a4 advance. White's bishop pair and the doubled c-pawns are the trade-off.}
11. Bb3 Bb7 12. Re1 Nbd7 13. Ba3 Nc5 14. Bxc5 Qxc5 15. Rad1 Rd8 16. c4 O-O 17. Nd2 b4
{An excellent practical decision. Black offers to trade queens while advancing the b-pawn, gaining queenside space. White's c4 pawn is a target if queens come off. The resulting ending will favour Black's active bishop and better pawn structure.}
18. h3 Nh5 19. g3 g6 20. Qe3 Ng7 21. Nf3 Ne6 22. Qxc5 Nxc5
{Queens are off. This is the ending Black wanted. The knight on c5 is superbly centralised, Black's king will go to the queenside, and the a4 advance will pick off White's pawns one by one. White's doubled c-pawns on an open file are a chronic weakness.}
23. Nd2 a5 24. c3 bxc3 25. Nf1 Bxe4 26. Re3 Nxb3 27. axb3 Bc2
{A beautiful endgame technique. Black's bishop on c2 paralyses White's queenside: it attacks the b3 pawn and prevents the rook from using the a-file. The passed a5 pawn, supported by the bishop and king, will prove decisive.}
28. Ra1 Bxb3 29. Rxc3 a4 30. Nd2 Rb8 31. Rb1 Rfc8 32. Ne4 Rb6 33. Nd2 Rb4 34. Ne4 Rbxc4 35. Rxc4 Rxc4 36. Nxd6 Rd4 37. Nb5 Rd1+ 38. Rxd1 Bxd1 39. Kf1 Bc2 40. Ke2 e4 41. Kd2 Bd3 42. Nc3 a3 43. Kc1 f5 44. h4 Kf7 45. Nd5 Ke6 46. Nf4+ Ke5 47. Ng2 Kd4
{The king marches in. With the a-pawn one step from queening and the e4 pawn controlling the critical squares, White has no defence. A model example of how the Kan queenside expansion translates into a won endgame.}
0-1

[Event "FIDE World U11 Open 2026"]
[Site "Roquetas de Mar ESP"]
[Date "2026.10.15"]
[Round "3.27"]
[White "Mistrekov, Dobrynia"]
[WhiteElo "1960"]
[Black "Hrybacz, Pawel"]
[BlackElo "1844"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Tag "Upset"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – Kan Sicilian: Black's View"]
[Opening "Kan/O'Kelly: ...e5, Bd3 – Black seizes open lines"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Nf3 Bb4 7. Bd2 d6 8. Bd3 Bxc3 9. Bxc3 Nbd7 10. O-O Nc5
{Black has repeated a familiar motif: exchange the Bb4 for the knight, and then occupy c5 with the other knight. The c5 square is the best outpost in the Kan – in the centre, protected by the a6-b5 pawn chain, and difficult to dislodge once Black castles. White can challenge it with Nd2-b3 but the knight will simply go to e6 or a4.}
11. Nd2 Na4 12. Kh1 Nxc3 13. bxc3 O-O 14. Qe2 Be6 15. Rad1 Rc8 16. c4 Qe7 17. Rb1 Rc7 18. f4 exf4 19. Rxf4 Nd7 20. Nb3 Ne5
{Black has achieved the ideal knight placement: Ne5 in the centre, reinforced by the bishop on e6. The rook on c7 protects the seventh rank and prepares ...Rfc8. White's queenside pawns are immobile and the c4 pawn will become a target.}
21. Na5 Rfc8 22. Rbf1 b6 23. Nb3 Bxc4 24. Nd4 Bxd3 25. cxd3 Rc1
{A devastating exchange. Black gives up the bishop pair to win the exchange tactically: the rook invades c1 with tempo, picking up the rook on f1 after the queen moves. Black's rook now dominates the open file.}
26. Nf5 Qe6 27. d4 Ng6 28. Rf2 b5
{The queenside expansion arrives at the perfect moment. White's pieces are tangled and Black's knight on g6 eyeing f4 and h4 provides attacking ideas. Black will double rooks on the c-file and march the b-pawn.}
29. Qb2 Rxf1+ 30. Rxf1 Qxe4 31. Nxd6 Rc2 32. Nxe4 Rxb2 33. d5 Re2 34. Nc5 Rd2 35. Nxa6 Rxd5 36. g3 Rd2 37. Rf5 Rxa2 38. Nb4 Ra5 39. Rd5 h6 40. Kg2 f6
{Black has converted the endgame advantage. The extra pawn on the queenside and active rook give a technically won position. The remaining moves are a model of rook endgame technique.}
41. Kh3 Ne5 42. Rd8+ Kf7 43. Rb8 Ke6 44. Rb6+ Kf5 45. Nd5 Nc4 46. Rb8 g5 47. Ne7+ Ke6 48. Nc6 Ra2 49. Rxb5 Ne3 50. Nd4+ Kf7 51. g4 Ra4 52. Rb3 Nxg4 53. Nf5 Nf2+ 54. Kg2 Ng4 55. h3 Kg6 56. Ne7+ Kf7 57. Rb7 Ne5 58. Nf5+ Kg6 59. Ne7+ Kh5 60. Nf5 Ra2+ 61. Kg3 Ra3+ 62. Kg2 Nd3 63. Rb6 Nf4+ 64. Kf1 Rf3+ 65. Kg1 Ne2+ 66. Kg2 Rxf5 67. Ra6 Nf4+ 68. Kg3 Kg6 69. Ra8 Rb5 70. Rg8+ Kf7 71. Ra8 Rb3+ 72. Kg4 h5+ 73. Kf5 Rb5+ 74. Ke4 Nxh3 75. Ra7+ Kg6 76. Ra8 h4 77. Rg8+ Kf7 78. Ra8 Nf4 79. Ra7+ Kg6 80. Ra6 h3 81. Kf3 h2 82. Ra1 Rb3+ 83. Kf2 Ra3 84. Ke1
{White resigns in a hopeless position. The h-pawn queens.}
0-1

[Event "Ganja Open 2026"]
[Site "Ganja AZE"]
[Date "2026.10.13"]
[Round "3.12"]
[White "Gopal Krishna Maheshwari"]
[WhiteElo "2040"]
[Black "Afandi, Sadagul"]
[BlackElo "1830"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Tag "Fighting"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – Kan Sicilian: Black's View"]
[Opening "Kan/O'Kelly: 5.Bd3 – Black misses the d5 break"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Bd3 e5 6. Nb3 Nc6 7. c3 Be7 8. O-O d6 9. Re1 O-O 10. c4 Be6 11. a3 Nd7 12. Nc3 Nc5 13. Nxc5 dxc5
{After 5.Bd3 White avoids the main Nc3 lines and aims for a quieter structural game. Here Black has traded the knight for c5, gaining the half-open d-file, but after dxc5 the pawn structure has changed character: Black now has a hanging pawn on c5 with no d5 break available. The weakness on d5 will be exploited.}
14. Nd5 Nd4 15. Be3 Bg5 16. b4
{The queenside break arrives quickly. White strikes at c5 before Black can consolidate the d4 knight. The bishop on g5 is misplaced – it should be on e7 supporting d6 and e5 – and Black's position begins to unravel.}
16... b6 17. Rb1 h6 18. Qh5 Bxd5 19. exd5 Re8 20. bxc5 bxc5 21. Rb7 Rf8 22. Bxg5 Qxg5 23. Qxg5 hxg5
{The queens have come off but White's activity is decisive. The rook on b7 is dominant, the passed d5 pawn is a monster, and Black's pieces are completely passive. This is the danger in the Kan with 5.Bd3: if Black plays too passively and allows White to fix the centre, the a6/b5 expansion never happens and the whole strategy falls apart.}
24. Rxe5 Rfe8 25. Rxe8+ Rxe8 26. f3 g6 27. Kf2 a5 28. Rc7 Nb3 29. Bc2 Rb8 30. Bxb3 Rxb3 31. Rxc5 Rxa3 32. d6 Rd3 33. Rc8+ Kg7 34. c5 Kf6 35. Ra8 Ke6 36. Rxa5 Rc3 37. Ke2 Rc2+ 38. Ke3 Rxg2 39. Ra7 Rc2 40. Re7+ Kf6 41. Kd4 Rc1 42. Re3 Rd1+ 43. Kc4 Rc1+ 44. Kd5 Rd1+ 45. Kc6 Rd2 46. d7 g4 47. fxg4 g5 48. Re8
{The d7 pawn queens next move. A cautionary tale about passive play in the Kan – the key lesson is that Black must actively seek the ...d5 break or ...b5 expansion before White's queenside pawns start rolling.}
1-0

[Event "Ganja Open 2026"]
