[White "Valiyeva, Asnad"]
[WhiteElo "1887"]
[Black "Khuduzade, Azad"]
[BlackElo "1931"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Tag "Fighting"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – Kan Sicilian: Black's View"]
[Opening "Kan/O'Kelly: ...e5, Nb3 – Black sacrifices the a-pawn for counterplay"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Nb3 Bb4 7. Bg5 d6 8. Bd3 O-O 9. O-O Bxc3 10. bxc3 Nbd7 11. Qe2 b5 12. a4 bxa4 13. Rxa4 Qc7
{A critical tabiya. Black has played the standard Kan queenside expansion (...b5) but White has sacrificed the a-file for activity. After 12.a4 bxa4 13.Rxa4, White has a rook bearing down on the a6 pawn. But Black does not try to hold the pawn – instead the queen comes to c7, keeping the bishop on a6 or b7 in reserve and maintaining the tension in the centre.}
14. Bd2 Bb7 15. Rfa1 d5 16. exd5 Bxd5 17. Rxa6 Rxa6 18. Bxa6
{White wins the a6 pawn, as planned. But Black has achieved full central equality: the bishop on d5 is powerful, the e5 pawn controls key squares, and the open a-file will belong to Black's remaining rook. This is a thematic Kan compensation – give the a-pawn, get the centre.}
18... h5 19. c4 Ba8 20. Be3 Ng4 21. f3 Nxe3 22. Qxe3 Nb6 23. Qe2 Rd8 24. Bb5 g6
{Black reorganises with precision. The knight goes to d5 via b6, the rooks connect, and Black's central control with e5/d5 gives full counterplay for the sacrificed pawn. The h5 advance also prepares ...h4 or simply discourages White from attacking on the kingside.}
25. Re1 f6 26. Rd1 Rxd1+ 27. Qxd1 Kg7 28. c5 Nd5 29. c4 Ne3 30. Qd2 Nf5 31. Qd7+ Qxd7 32. Bxd7 Kf7 33. Kf2 Ke7 34. Bb5 Kd8 35. Nd2 Kc7 36. g3 g5 37. Be8 h4
{An instructive endgame. Black has a powerful knight on f5 and the h-pawn is advancing. The game will simplify to a draw after Black's knight and h-pawn create enough counterplay against White's passed c-pawn. Both sides play precisely.}
38. gxh4 gxh4 39. Bf7 Bc6 40. Ne4 Bxe4 41. fxe4 Nd4 42. Bd5 Kd7 43. Kg2 Kc7 44. Kh3 Nf3 45. Kg4 Nxh2+ 46. Kxh4 Nf3+ 47. Kh5 Kd7 48. c6+ Ke7 49. c5 Nd4 50. Kg6 Nb5 51. Kg7 Nc7 52. Bc4 Ne8+ 53. Kg6 Nc7 54. Kf5 Ne8 55. Bd5 Nc7 56. Kg4 Ne8 57. Kf3 Nc7 58. Ke3 Kd8 59. Kd3 Ne8 60. c7+ Kxc7 61. Kc4 Ng7 62. Kb5 f5 63. exf5 Nxf5 64. Be4 Ng3 65. Bf3 Nf1 66. Kb4 Nd2 67. Bd5 e4 68. Kc3 e3 69. Kd3 Nf1 70. Ke2 Ng3+ 71. Kxe3 Nf5+ 72. Kf4 Nd4 73. Ke5 Nb5 74. Ke4
{A hard-fought draw. The e-pawn queens just in time to hold. Black's strategy of sacrificing the a-pawn for central control was completely sound – the draw was a fair result.}
1/2-1/2

[Event "Pan-Am Seniors +50 2026"]
