[White "Pineda, J"]
[WhiteElo "1945"]
[Black "Santanna, P"]
[BlackElo "2107"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Tag "Upset"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – Kan Sicilian: Black's View"]
[Opening "Kan/O'Kelly: ...e5, Nb3 Bb4 Qf3 – endgame after queen trade"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Nb3 Bb4 7. Qf3 d6 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. Qxf6 Bxc3+
{An unusual queen trade early in the opening. White plays Qf3, eyeing a direct attack, but after the bishop exchange on f6 both queens disappear. The position is an endgame from move 10, but Black has excellent compensation: the bishop pair, active pieces, and the e5 centre. The doubled c-pawns on the c-file are White's weakness.}
11. bxc3 gxf6 12. O-O-O Ke7 13. Bc4 Rg8 14. g3 Nd7 15. Bd5 a5
{Black ignores the kingside structure and goes queenside immediately. The a5 advance will support ...Nb6 targeting the weak d5 bishop. Black's king, safely centralised after the early queens-off, actively participates in the endgame – a theme unique to this variation.}
16. Nd2 Nb6 17. Nc4 Nxd5 18. exd5 Bg4 19. Rd2 Rac8 20. Nxa5 Rxc3
{A clean tactical decision. Black sacrifices the a5 pawn to activate the rook immediately. After 20...Rxc3 the pressure on c2 and c7 compensates for the material. White's queenside pawns are doubled and vulnerable; Black's active pieces more than compensate.}
21. Kb2 Rc5 22. Nb3 Rc4 23. Re1 Kd7 24. Re3 Rgc8 25. Na5 Ra4 26. Ra3 Rxa3 27. Kxa3
{After the rook trade, White's knight is tied to defending the queenside. Black's two rooks have been replaced by active coordination between king, rook, and pawns. The d5 pawn is a target. A draw by repetition follows as neither side can make progress without risk.}
1/2-1/2

[Event "Titled Tue 13th Oct 2026"]
[Site "chess.com INT"]
[Date "2026.10.13"]
[Round "3"]
[White "Ahmed Mustafa, Faris"]
[WhiteElo "2118"]
[Black "Sobek, J"]
[BlackElo "2176"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Tag "Fighting"]
[Annotator "TWIC 1667 – Kan Sicilian: Black's View"]
[Opening "Kan/O'Kelly: 5.Bd3 e5 6.Nf3 – Black opens the centre, endgame battle"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Bd3 e5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O d5 8. exd5 Nxd5 9. Be4 Be6 10. Ng5 Nf6 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 12. Nxe6 Qxd1 13. Nxg7+ Bxg7 14. Rxd1
{A sharp sequence. Black played 7...d5 – the thematic central break that the Kan always aims for. After a series of forced exchanges, queens are off early and Black has an interesting endgame: two bishops, but doubled c-pawns and a displaced king. White has the rook pair.}
14... Nd5 15. a3 a5 16. c4 Nb6 17. b3 a4 18. Be3 Nd7 19. b4 Ke7 20. Ra2 Rhb8 21. Rad2 Rb7 22. Nc3 f5
{Black activates with ...f5, supporting the e5 pawn and preventing f2-f4. The plan is to use the bishop pair and the queenside pawn majority (a4, doubled c-pawns aren't entirely bad here) to generate activity. The centralised king helps.}
23. f3 e4 24. Bd4 Bxd4+ 25. Rxd4 exf3 26. gxf3 Nb6 27. Re1+ Kf7 28. Re5 Kf6 29. f4 Rg8+ 30. Kf2 Rbg7 31. Rd6+ Kf7 32. Rxc6 Rg2+ 33. Ke3 Rxh2 34. Rxb6
{White wins the b6 knight, gaining a decisive material advantage. Black has been unable to coordinate the rooks on the g-file into a real attack. The position illustrates a key Kan lesson: the central break ...d5 is correct and necessary, but the resulting endgame requires accurate play. One inaccuracy in the piece coordination and White's rooks take over.}
34... Rg3+ 35. Kd4 Rd2+ 36. Kc5 Rxc3 37. Rxf5+ Ke7 38. Rb7+ Ke8 39. Rg5 Kf8 40. Rd5 Rxd5+ 41. Kxd5 Rxa3 42. c5 Rc3 43. c6 a3 44. Ra7
{White's connected passed pawns on c6 and b4 are unstoppable. A good illustration of how precise rook-and-pawn technique wins these queenless middlegames – Black's a-pawn runs out of steam while c6 promotes.}
1-0
